Ok, guys: about characters who carry around iron balls... It's always 
occurred to me that being punished really isn't as bad as it ought to be...
So you have to skimp a little on inventory, so what? I'm probably as much a
pack-rat as any Nethack player I know, and even while being punished most of
my characters still have ample carrying capacity left over, once they drop 
their "junk". I mean, how many "extra" black caps and/or gems does one need, 
"just on the off chance" that they may find a store soon, or get rusted? 
Compare punishment with other "inconveniences" such as lycanthropy and 
polymorph traps. How much carrying capacity does one usually end up with then? 
And punishment is more avoidable than these.

And let us not forget that there is an "up" side to punishment also... I assume
that at this point in the development of the game everyone knows that an iron
ball makes a very damaging weapon (I think there is even a rumor to that 
effect). Well, maybe it is a minimal spoiler to mention that is even more 
damaging, in fact, than just about any non-magical weapon. Of course, you 
can't enchant it, and it confers no "special" abilities, but it also never 
*loses* plusses because of rust or curse, and never gets welded to your hand, 
thus getting in the way of pick-axes, gloves, rings, etc.. The only way to get 
one of these is to be punished, or find one in a bones file. For those who can 
carry and wield one, it compares favorably to any normal weapon, up to and 
including a +0 two-handed sword...

Okay, so assuming that punishment has some room for augmentation (I suppose you
were wondering why I brought all this up); I was in the midst of fixing some
bugs related to level-teleporting, falling down stairs, and, of course, carrying
the iron ball, when I decided that the whole falling-down-stairs-while-punished
scenario should probably be re-thought. Consequently, I have mailed some 
changes to the development group for their approval. These may or may not, in
part or in their entirety, be sneaking into your systems in an imminent patch 
level.

This is the new scenario:

	When punished, one has 2 leg bands on, attached by a short chain. This
	arrangement makes normal descent of stairs impossible, as one will 
	*always* trip at some point on the stairs. (This is my rationalization
	of the "automatic" fall when punished. There is no way to actually see 
	or use the leg bands or short chain, though. If someone can think of a 
	use for these, maybe they could be added as real items...)

	When falling, one *always* drops the ball. Seems intuitive to me. 
	(Kids, don't try this at home).

	Once falling, there are chances of being 'hit' by the ball (old news),
	or 'dragged down the stairs' (brand spanking new concept). The relative
	chances of each, and the effects, depend on how and at what point the
	character trips and drops the ball:

		If the ball drops "forward", there is little to no chance of 
		being hit by the ball (no spoilers here: if you want the exact
		probability, you would have to look at the source, or talk to
		your local Nethack guru), but a "good" chance of being dragged 
		down the stairs. Dragging damage is maximum, since the dragging
		presumably started relatively early in the descent.
		
		If the ball doesn't drop "forward", there is a good chance that
		you will get hit by the ball. Semi-independently, there is a 
		chance that you will get dragged down. I say 'semi-' because, 
		once one has been hit, I assumed that the ball is a fair way 
		down the stairs, so I reduced the probability of being
		subsequently dragged. 

		Whether the ball falls "forward" depends on how you transport
		the ball, i.e. wielding, carrying or dragging. Without giving 
		anything away, just use your common sense. I will mention that 
		I assumed that the character would not try to carry the ball 
		in one hand if he/she didn't have to...
		
	When being dragged, there are probabilities that certain "loose" items
	will be dropped on the stairs. When dropping items this way, there is
	a relatively small chance that any particular dropped item will get 
	left on the *previous* level. The chance is relatively small, because 
	a) the character has a chance of not even tripping before reaching the 
	halfway point, and b) even if an item is dropped high up on the stairs,
	it could roll or tumble down.
		
	The messages that accompany these new occurrences are somewhat numerous
	and maybe a little verbose. I have done this so that Nethack veterans
	will be able to clearly see what is being perpetrated against their
	beloved characters, instead of just "spacing" past the verbiage, only
	to discover that they are barely alive and practically naked, their
	precious items strewn throughout the dungeon. The messages can be 
	reduced in number and/or "tersified" once people are used to this stuff.

All of this is the product of a warped mind left to roam. I do believe, 
however, that it provides the best tradeoff in variety/interest and realism.
Note also that characters' destinies can be controlled somewhat by making 
careful decisions about ball transport. We will see if the development team 
agrees... Whatever the actual code changes (if any), I would appeciate some 
discussion...
